+# BLOCKED: 002_unique_email.sql cannot be applied to production
+
+## What is required
+
+`users.email` must be `NOT NULL` and unique case-insensitively.
+
+## Why it is impossible without touching customer rows
+
+`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` contains data that already violates both rules:
+
+| Problem | Rows |
+| --- | --- |
+| `email IS NULL` | id 17 (`Goran Okafor`) |
+| Case-insensitive duplicate | ids 1 (`ana.silva@example.com`), 4 (`Ana.Silva@example.com`), 23 (`ana.silva@example.com`) |
+
+SQLite cannot add `NOT NULL` or a `UNIQUE` index to a table containing such
+rows. `migrations/002_unique_email.sql` rebuilds the table and creates
+`UNIQUE INDEX users_email_unique ON users (lower(email))`; on the production
+snapshot that migration fails:
+
+- copying id 17 fails the `NOT NULL` constraint;
+- even with the NULL removed, ids 1, 4 and 23 collide on `lower(email)`.
+
+The only ways to make the migration succeed against the current data are to
+delete/merge the duplicate rows, rewrite or invent values for them, or soften
+the constraint (partial index, filtered rows, insert-only triggers). All of
+these either modify Support-owned customer data or leave the table not
+actually `NOT NULL`/unique, so none were done. The fixture snapshot was also
+left untouched.
+
+## What Support needs to do first
+
+1. Decide the canonical row for ids 1, 4 and 23 (keep one, and give the others
+   distinct email addresses or deactivate/delete them per Support's policy).
+2. Supply a valid email for id 17, or deactivate/delete that account.
+3. Re-run `migrations/002_unique_email.sql` once no NULL or case-insensitive
+   duplicate emails remain. No further change to the migration is needed.
+
+## Current state
+
+- `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` passes: an empty database gets `NOT NULL` plus
+  the case-insensitive unique index.
+- `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` fails as expected on the legacy rows above;
+  it was not worked around.
+- Migration does not `UPDATE` or `DELETE` any rows; the rebuild copies values
+  verbatim.
